module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.heal_converge_overruns_the_90_minute_cap_on_a_contended_runner_when_the_rebuilt_seed_round_pages

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { BelowCeiling, FoundOnRung, RecurringFailureMode, RungRead }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { MechanicallyPreventable, Mitigatable }
import gunbc.rung_climb { ClimbableButUnbuilt }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }

data heal_converge_overruns_the_90_minute_cap_on_a_contended_runner_when_the_rebuilt_seed_round_pages: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "heal_converge_overruns_the_90_minute_cap_on_a_contended_runner_when_the_rebuilt_seed_round_pages" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungRead { found_at: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable }, standing: BelowCeiling { current: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable }, ceiling: MechanicallyPreventable, blocker: ClimbableButUnbuilt, trigger_capability: "Enforce gunbc.host.memory_envelope (memory.high throttles early, memory.max refuses late, MemorySwapMax 0 refuses an overrun rather than paging it) around the heal converge seed builds, and enroll an admission read of each runner unit against its declared runner_slot_desired that refuses a heal run landing on a drifted or over-committed unit. This is an enforcement that has to execute and then STAY ENROLLED -- deployed-but-not-enrolled is invisible -- which is why the ceiling is mechanically preventable and not higher. Sufficient for: the memory-heaviest rebuilt-seed round is bounded (refused fast) or placed on a measured-fast, less-contended unit, so it cannot silently consume the 90-minute budget. It does not touch the 90-minute cap itself, which is the operator's to set." } },

  receipts: [
    "**heal converge overruns the 90-minute cap on a contended self-hosted runner** (an environmental timeout read as a compiler defect). The heal-generated-artifacts step 'Converge the stage0 mirrors to the seed's own fixed point' runs `claim_executor --regen-round-cost --base-mirror` and is capped at 90 minutes; a healthy run finishes the converge step in ~33-36 minutes (job ~42-45). When it blows the cap the cheap read is 'the regen hung' or 'this PR's mirrors are expensive'. Neither holds; the work is byte-identical, only the wall-clock per phase moves, because the job runs on the shared self-hosted pool (machines srv3, srv4). What is MEASURED is that wall-clock scales with which runner unit the job lands on; what is INFERRED (and the leading, not confirmed, hypothesis) is the mechanism behind one of the two signatures.",

    "IT IS NOT A HANG. The convergence round count is bounded by `v1_compiler.required_regen_host::regen_convergence_generation_bound`, so there is no unbounded loop to hang in. The '55 minutes of silence' that made run 41240308313 (PR #15527, head 2f3cd05e, runner srv3-12-1793479415-4005434) look frozen at `round.built_seed_regen` was the CHILD's stderr being discarded: `run_built_seed_regen` used to spawn `spawn_rebuilt_seed_regen_child` on `Command::output()`, which buffers and drops the child's per-phase trace, so a ~53-minute phase sat silent in the job log while the child worked. That blind spot is already repaired on main by gunb-ai/gunbc#15594 (commit f64821c7), which forwards the child with `RebuiltSeedChildOutput::ForwardToJobLog`; the killed builds predate it (heads 2f3cd05e dated 2026-10-31, ce61ceb7 dated 2026-10-29; the fix merged 2026-11-01). A silent `built_seed_regen` on a post-#15594 head would be a different, real finding.",

    "IT IS NOT A COST REGRESSION ON INPUTS. The regen plan is byte-identical across the killed and the good runs -- `planned=176 executed=176 adjudicated=176` on all four specimens below, no mirror or round added. The extra time is not more work; it is the same cargo/rustc-bound phases at greater wall-clock.",

    "TWO KILLED RUNS, TWO SIGNATURES, BOTH HOST-SIDE BUT PROBABLY NOT THE SAME RESOURCE -- and the good runs bracket them. (a) UNIFORM slowdown, run 40938516623 (PR #15388 head ce61ceb7, runner srv4-05-1793363381-2383450, machine srv4): EVERY phase moved together -- `round.seed_build` 17 min and 12 min, `compile.emit` 11 min, `compile.reconcile` 3 min, required-regen `elapsed_ms` 1342703 -- against healthy 4-5 min / 4-5 min / ~70 s / ~590000-650000 (good runs cb5fd8ba on srv4-10 and a0a17eb7 on srv4-09). When the non-memory phases scale by the same factor as the memory phases, the signature is whole-host pressure (CPU/scheduler or I/O bandwidth on a co-loaded unit), and paging is NOT needed to explain it. (b) TARGETED stall, run 41240308313 (head 2f3cd05e, runner srv3-12-1793479415-4005434, machine srv3): the first ~30 minutes ran at NORMAL speed (`round.seed_build` 4 min, `compile.emit` 5 min, required-regen `elapsed_ms` 662259 -- indistinguishable from a good run), and only then did the single memory-heaviest phase, `round.built_seed_regen` (a seed rebuild plus a full second `--required-regen` in the child), consume the entire remaining ~55 minutes. Same code, same 176/176/176 plan, split by the runner unit.",

    "THE MECHANISM FOR SIGNATURE (b) IS AN INFERENCE, NOT A MEASUREMENT. On srv3 the early phases were at healthy speed while only the memory-heaviest phase ballooned, which is what a memory-overcommitted, swapping host does to a large rustc/cargo allocation. That is the LEADING HYPOTHESIS, not a read fact. What is measured is only that this phase took ~5x its healthy wall-clock on srv3. The step emits `memory-cgroup-bind: not requested` (`v1_compiler.memory_governor::CgroupBindDecision::NotRequested`), so no bound-envelope receipt and no swap figure was recorded in the job log for the window, and the run is two days old so a worker (no host reach) cannot attribute cumulative host counters to it. THE READING THAT WOULD SETTLE IT: `memory.swap.peak` and PSI memory `avg10/avg60` on the `gunbc-runner-srv3-*` systemd unit for the 2026-10-31T20:49-22:15 window (or, forward-looking, the same memory-envelope receipt `gunbc.host.memory_envelope` would emit once `memory-cgroup-bind` is requested instead of NotRequested) -- swap growth with no proportional CPU progress confirms paging; flat swap with saturated CPU reclassifies (b) as the same CPU/scheduler contention as (a). DESIGN (fabric/compute) is the reason paging is the leading guess: it records that the isolation authority for this pool is the SLOT plus the memory envelope (memory.high/memory.max/`MemorySwapMax 0`, which REFUSES an overrun rather than paging it), and that live runner units DRIFT from that declared shape -- a `MemorySwapMax` of 32 GiB where the slot declares 0, read on the fleet 2026-10-21, the class at `gunbc.recurring_failure_mode` `the_live_runner_unit_memory_shape_drifts_from_the_declared_slot`. An unbound build on a drifted unit is exactly the configuration that would page; it is not yet shown to have done so on these runs.",

    "A SECOND, INPUT-TRIGGERED AMPLIFIER PUSHES THE TIPPING POINT CLOSER. Whether the extra round runs at all is decided by `first_generation_equal` = `sync.matches && hand.unverifiable.is_empty()` in `v1_compiler.required_regen_host::run_regen_round_cost`: when the first regenerated generation is not yet the fixed point (`first_generation_equal = false`), the step pays `round.rebuild_from_installed` + `round.install` + `round.built_seed_regen` -- a whole extra `--required-regen` in the child, ~11-15 min at healthy speed -- on top of the ~40-minute baseline. The two good runs had `first_generation_equal = true` and skipped it; run 2f3cd05e (the srv3 case) had `false` and paid it. So a pull request whose own seed is not immediately a fixed point has only ~35-40 minutes of headroom before the cap, and any host slowdown -- which the srv3/srv4 pool delivers under load -- tips it over. This amplifier is orthogonal to which resource is contended.",

    "WHY IT IS NOT A FAILING CHANGE: the killed heads (2f3cd05e on PR #15527, ce61ceb7 on PR #15388) carry no defect that would slow their own regeneration; a re-run of the same head on an unloaded unit (srv4-09, srv4-10 are where the good runs landed) completes the converge step in ~33-36 minutes. Treating a cap overrun as a defect in the pull request invites the DESIGN section 5 concealment -- weakening the change, or reaching to raise the cap, to hide a host-side deficit.",

    "CEILING: mechanically preventable, NOT structurally guaranteed. The remedy is an enforcement that has to RUN and then STAY ENROLLED -- bind the memory envelope on the heal builds and enroll an admission read that refuses a drifted or over-committed unit. Enforced-but-not-enrolled is invisible (the very `the_live_runner_unit_memory_shape_drifts_from_the_declared_slot` row is a deployed-but-not-enrolled failure of that kind), so the invalid state -- a heal converge page-thrashing past the cap -- does have an accepted program today: a heal workflow deployed without the envelope on a drifted unit. That is the section 4d boundary between mechanically preventable and structurally guaranteed, and I cannot cross it: no language-level fact makes the bad event unprogrammatic here, only a verifier that, when present and enrolled, refuses it. So the rung climb is Mitigatable to MechanicallyPreventable, blocked by the envelope-and-admission instrument being ClimbableButUnbuilt.",

    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: bind the memory envelope around the heal converge seed builds and schedule the rebuilt-seed round on a heal-reserved or measured-fast unit, and gate heal admission on a live read of each runner unit against its declared `gunbc.runner.runner_slot_desired` (refusing a unit whose `MemorySwapMax` has drifted from the declared 0). To turn signature (b)'s inference into a fact, first read `memory.swap.peak`/PSI memory on the `gunbc-runner-srv3-*` unit for that window (or emit the bound-envelope receipt forward, once the bind is requested); swap-without-CPU confirms paging, flat-swap-with-saturated-CPU folds (b) into (a). Until the trigger runs, heal convergence wall-clock rests on a declared authority that DESIGN already says is not fully installed.",

    "REVIEW TELL: a heal-generated-artifacts cancel at the 90-minute cap on a head whose regen plan size (planned/executed/adjudicated) matches a known-green run's, and whose job log shows normal-speed early phases but a `round.built_seed_regen` (the srv3 signature) or a uniformly slow `round.seed_build` / `compile.emit` (the srv4-05 signature) -- read the runner name and `elapsed_ms` before reading the diff, and read `memory-cgroup-bind` before blaming the page.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.host.memory_envelope", decl_name: "memory_envelope_enforcement", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.the_live_runner_unit_memory_shape_drifts_from_the_declared_slot", decl_name: "the_live_runner_unit_memory_shape_drifts_from_the_declared_slot", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.heal_workflow", decl_name: "heal_workflow_required_bins", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ],
}
